{
  "code": "template",
  "name": "",
  "english": "",
  "font": "",
  "strings": {
    "Bots keep their bags": "",
    "mod-playerbots empties the bags of every random bot every 10 to 40 minutes and after every death. Without this change the bots never have anything to sell. Food, drink and potions are still renewed.": "",
    "No crash on level-scaled loot": "",
    "Since CoA core #6403 a bot that loots a level-scaled item ends the server. Left out by itself as soon as mod-playerbots has the fix.": "",
    "Bots gather on slopes": "",
    "mod-playerbots ignores every vein and herb that lies more than 3.5 yards above or below the bot. Veins are in hillsides, so miners never mined. Now the height may grow with the distance.": "",
    "Bots go for the veins and herbs they see": "",
    "mod-playerbots only gathers what lies within the distance a bot walks for a corpse. A gatherer now goes for every vein and herb in sight, as a player follows the dots on the minimap.": "",
    "One answer to WTS, not fifty": "",
    "mod-playerbots lets every bot that hears \"WTS [item]\" whisper a price it does not mean. With deals by chat enabled, the few bots that really want the item answer instead.": "",
    "Hand-to-hand trades are the module's": "",
    "A deal agreed on by chat can be traded in person. mod-playerbots would run its own rules on that trade window (and cancel or accept it by its own prices); for a bot the module is trading for, it stands back.": "",
    "Bots only gather ore and herbs they pass within this distance. With the default of 15 yards hardly any reach the auction house.": "",
    "Bots were handed better gear at every level-up, so none ever needed anything from the auction house - and sold the old pieces to a vendor.": "",
    "New bots start in uncommon gear at most. Rare gear becomes something worth buying.": "",
    "In a group, bots roll need on what is an upgrade for them.": "",
    "mod-playerbots' own chatter - a report of every quest and kill, dungeon suggestions, canned replies - is replaced by the personalities of this module.": "",
    "Switches the module on.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "Writes to the server log what the bots do: every trip, auction, bid, purchase\n    and crafted item, and every five minutes a summary: what the bots carry, what they gathered (ore, herbs,\n    hides, cloth), which professions they have and what comes of their recipes.\nDefault:     0 - Disabled\n    1 - Enabled": "",
    "Bots sell and buy at an auctioneer, like a player. This is how close (yards)\n    they have to stand.\nDefault:     35": "",
    "Bots below this level do not use the auction house.\nDefault:     1": "",
    "A bot at the auction house empties its auction mail: the money of sold items,\n    the items nobody bought and what it bought. Without this that mail piles up,\n    because the bots never open a mailbox for it.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "More is going on in the evening and at the weekend than at night, by the\n    server's clock: fewer bots set out for the auction house at four in the morning.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "The bots remember what things sold for and what came back unsold. With this\n    option that memory survives a restart, in two small tables of the characters\n    database (mod_playerbots_auctions_market, mod_playerbots_auctions_unsold).\n    They are created when the server starts.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (the market starts from scratch at every restart)": "",
    "Auctions run out by the clock on the wall, also while the server is off. On a\n    server that is only started for an evening, most auctions are gone by the next\n    evening and the auction house starts empty every day. With this option every\n    auction - of bots and of players - gets back the time the server was off when\n    it starts: an auction put up for 24 hours runs for 24 hours of server time.\n    The module notes once a minute that the server is running, in a small table\n    of the characters database (mod_playerbots_auctions_state). On a server that\n    runs around the clock this changes nothing worth mentioning.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "Percent of the bots that never use the auction house and sell everything to a\n    vendor, as many players do.\nDefault:     15": "",
    "How often the module looks at the bots.\nDefault:     30": "",
    "How many bots may do their business per look. Keeps the load even with\n    thousands of bots.\nDefault:     10": "",
    "A bot that was at the auctioneers and stays near them does its business again\n    after a random time between these two values - times 0.4 for a bot that lives\n    in the auction house, up to times 1.6 for one that hardly uses it.\nDefault:     20 and 60": "",
    "Trips To the Auction House": "",
    "Bots set out for the auction house on their own.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (bots only do their business when they happen to pass an auctioneer)": "",
    "After a trip a bot waits a random time between these two values before the\n    next one - times 0.5 for a bot that lives in the auction house, up to times\n    1.35 for one that hardly uses it.\nDefault:     60 and 180": "",
    "A bot sets out when it carries this many different things to sell. Every bot\n    draws its own line between ItemsMin (tidy bots) and ItemsMax (bots that\n    collect for long). Keep both low: mod-playerbots sells the same things to\n    any vendor a bot passes, so bots rarely carry many. A bot that is in town\n    anyway goes with a single thing, and full bags always send it off.\nDefault:     2": "",
    "See ItemsMin: the line of the bots that collect the longest.\nDefault:     8": "",
    "Lowest quality of weapons and armor that is sold.\nDefault:     2": "",
    "Lowest quality of everything else (trade goods, consumables, recipes, ...).\nDefault:     1": "",
    "Highest quality that is sold: 2 uncommon, 3 rare, 4 epic, 5 legendary. Whatever\n    can be traded may be offered, so the default leaves it open at the top.\nDefault:     5": "",
    "Item classes that are sold, separated by commas.\n    0 consumable, 1 container, 2 weapon, 3 gem, 4 armor, 5 reagent, 6 projectile,\n    7 trade goods, 9 recipe, 11 quiver, 13 key, 15 miscellaneous, 16 glyph\n    12 quest (only what can be traded - most of it cannot), 15 includes companion\n    pets and mounts. Projectiles are left out: bots are handed their ammunition.\nDefault:     \"0,1,2,3,4,5,7,9,11,12,13,15,16\"": "",
    "Items that are never sold, separated by commas.\nDefault:     \"\"": "",
    "Items whose name contains one of these texts are never sold (upper and lower\n    case do not matter). Keeps test and technical items out of the auction house.\n    The texts are separated by commas and taken exactly as written, spaces\n    included: \" test \" only matches the single word.\nDefault:     \"monster -,deprecated,[ph],(test), test ,qatest,qa ,zzold,npc equip,unused\"": "",
    "Bots pay the normal deposit, get it back when the item sells and lose it if\n    nobody buys - as players do. It does not decide what is offered.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (bots list for free)": "",
    "An auction that would bring less than this is not created. Low-level bots\n    carry little of value: with a higher value they hardly sell anything.\nDefault:     1": "",
    "Where mod-playerbots lets bots sell to vendors, it sells whatever a bot does\n    not need - also what would be worth an auction. With this the bots do not\n    give a vendor what is meant for the auction house (worth at least\n    MinAuctionValueSilver, not offered in vain too often) or the materials they\n    have plans for. Bots that never use the auction house and bots travelling\n    with a player are left alone. Needs CityTrip.Enable: bots that never get to\n    an auctioneer keep nothing.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "A bot standing next to a vendor sells it what it has no use for and what is\n    not worth an auction: grey things, plain equipment, what nobody wanted at\n    the auction house. Random bots of mod-playerbots do not sell to vendors by\n    themselves; without this their bags fill up with junk.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "Bots stop selling in an auction house that holds this many auctions.\nDefault:     20000": "",
    "Factor on the vendor value, per quality of the item.\nDefault:     1.5": "",
    "Factor on the vendor value, per quality of the item.\nDefault:     3": "",
    "Factor on the vendor value, per quality of the item.\nDefault:     6": "",
    "Factor on the vendor value, per quality of the item.\nDefault:     12": "",
    "Factor on the vendor value, per quality of the item.\nDefault:     25": "",
    "Factor on the vendor value, per quality of the item.\nDefault:     50": "",
    "What auctions really sold for moves what the bots consider the usual price of\n    an item, for selling and for buying. Only sales a bot took part in count, an\n    item needs three sales before its price is \"known\", and every sale is cut\n    down to between half and three times the calculated price.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (always vendor value times quality factor)": "",
    "No bot sells for less than the vendor value times this factor, however low\n    the other offers are - neither as buyout price nor as starting bid.\nDefault:     1.5": "",
    "Highest buyout price of one auction, in gold. A bot keeps an item that would\n    be worth more, and does not pay more than this for an auction. 0 = no limit.\nDefault:     5000": "",
    "Bots buy and bid.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (bots only sell)": "",
    "How many randomly picked items on offer a bot looks at per visit.\nDefault:     60": "",
    "Bots buy the auctions of players.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "Bots buy the auctions of other bots.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "Bots also bid instead of only buying outright. A bid can outbid a player.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "Bots pay with their own money and cannot buy what they cannot afford. Thrifty\n    bots keep a reserve.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (bots always have enough money; this brings new gold into the world)": "",
    "For something it wants, a bot is always willing to pay at least the vendor\n    value times this factor, so selling to the bots is never worse than selling to\n    a vendor.\nDefault:     1.2": "",
    "For items a vendor sells, bots pay at most this percent of the vendor's price.\n    Otherwise players could buy from a vendor and sell to the bots for a profit.\nDefault:     75": "",
    "Bots craft.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "For a recipe that needs an anvil, a forge, a cooking fire or the like, the bot\n    walks to the nearest one in the capital it is in, makes its things there and\n    walks back to the auctioneers to sell them.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (such recipes are left out)": "",
    "Bots buy the materials a recipe is missing when the product is worth it.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (bots only craft from what they carry)": "",
    "Materials a vendor sells for money in any quantity (thread, vials, flux, ...)\n    the bot buys from the vendor at the vendor's price when it crafts.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (recipes that need such materials only work if the bot carries them)": "",
    "The product has to be worth this many percent more than its materials.\nDefault:     10": "",
    "A recipe that still raises the bot's skill is worth this many percent more to\n    it, so bots also craft at a loss to get better - as players do who level a\n    profession. Most crafted things are worth less than what goes into them; with\n    a low value smiths and tailors hardly make anything.\nDefault:     100": "",
    "A bot does not pay any price for a material just because the product is worth\n    a lot: offers above this factor times the usual price of the material are left\n    alone.\nDefault:     1.5": "",
    "How many of its recipes a bot thinks through per visit.\nDefault:     40": "",
    "The most a producer makes of one thing in one go.\nDefault:     5": "",
    "Jewelcrafters prospect ore, scribes mill herbs and enchanters disenchant gear\n    they have no use for - when what comes out on average is worth more than\n    what goes in, or when they need it for their own recipes.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "Enchanters put their enchantments on vellum and sell the scrolls - the way an\n    enchantment gets into the auction house. They need the materials, their rod and\n    a vellum, which scribes make. A scroll is priced by what goes into it.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "Scribes make glyphs. The glyphs of Wrath of the Lich King change the spells of\n    the old classes; the classes of Conquest of Azeroth cannot use them, so nobody\n    buys them there. Without glyphs scribes make inks, scrolls, vellum, cards and\n    books. Enable on a server with the old classes.\nDefault:     0 - Disabled\n    1 - Enabled": "",
    "Deals By Chat": "",
    "Bots answer WTB and WTS lines of players.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "How many bots answer one line at the most (1 to 10). They answer one after the\n    other, a few seconds apart, each with its own price.\nDefault:     3": "",
    "The channels the bots read, as parts of their names separated by commas, for\n    example \"general, trade\". Empty: every channel.\nDefault:     \"\"": "",
    "A bot that is close to the player says so with its offer; \"meet\" then takes\n    the offer and brings the bot over. A bot that was answered with \"yes\" asks\n    whether to meet instead of using the mail. On \"meet\" it joins the player's group for\n    the way (that is how a bot finds a player), walks over, opens the trade\n    window, puts in what was agreed and accepts when the other side holds what\n    was agreed as well. Then it leaves the group again. If it cannot get there\n    within two minutes, or the trade is closed, the deal goes through the mail.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (always the mail)": "",
    "How close the bot has to be to offer that, in yards, on the same map and\n    outside of dungeons. A large value (1000 and more) lets nearly every bot of\n    the zone offer it, which is handy for trying it out - the walk can then take\n    longer than the two minutes a bot gives itself. 5000 at the most.\nDefault:     150": "",
    "How long the parcel of a bot takes to arrive, in seconds. -1: as long as mail\n    between players takes on this server (Mail.DeliveryDelay in worldserver.conf,\n    one hour unless changed). 0: at once.\nDefault:     -1": "",
    "Auctioneers offer the trading post. Disabled, a click on an auctioneer\n    opens the auction house as always. Goods already bid on still arrive.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "Players get the post's flyer once a day at their first login.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "Once a week players may collect a free sample (always junk) at an auctioneer.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "The post sells a material while the auction house holds fewer pieces of it\n    than this, all auctions together. With 1, a single auction of it is enough to\n    take it off the post's shelves; with 10, the post goes on selling while the\n    auction house has only a handful. What crafters make is sold only while the\n    auction house has none of it. The shelves follow the auction house when the\n    post is opened and every 15 seconds while somebody looks at them.\nDefault:     1\n    0 - The post sells everything it knows, whatever the auction house holds": "",
    "The buyout - delivered at once - as a multiple of what the thing usually goes\n    for among the bots (the same value their own prices start from). 1 to 100.\nDefault:     3.0 for materials, 4.0 for what crafters make": "",
    "The lowest bid - delivered when the auction ends - in the same way. Kept below\n    the buyout. 1 to 100.\nDefault:     2.0 for materials, 2.5 for what crafters make": "",
    "How many pieces of one thing each post has a day, for all players together.\n    Raise it with the number of players on the server. The day ends at midnight,\n    server time.\nDefault:     60 of a material, 10 of a crafted thing": "",
    "A character buys one lot of a thing a day; this is the most pieces that lot\n    can have (1 to 100; never more than a stack). Lots of 1, 5 and this many are\n    on offer. What enchants one particular piece of equipment for good - the\n    scroll of an enchantment, a leg armor, a spellthread, a belt buckle - and\n    what one may only carry once are sold one at a time.\nDefault:     20 of a material, 5 of a crafted thing": "",
    "The best quality the post sells: 1 common (white), 2 uncommon (green),\n    3 rare (blue), 4 epic (purple).\nDefault:     3": "",
    "Items the post never sells, separated by commas. The default keeps out what\n    is meant to be scarce: materials made on a cooldown (Arcanite Bar, Mooncloth,\n    Cured Rugged Hide, Primal Mooncloth, Primal Might, Spellcloth, Shadowcloth,\n    Titansteel Bar, Ebonweave, Moonshroud, Spellweave) and the orbs of the\n    Northrend dungeons and raids (Frozen Orb, Runed Orb, Crusader Orb, Primordial\n    Saronite). The items of PlayerbotsAuctions.Sell.ExcludedItemIDs and the\n    names of PlayerbotsAuctions.Sell.ExcludedNameParts are kept out as well.\nDefault:     \"12360,14342,15407,21845,23571,24271,24272,37663,41593,41594,41595,43102,45087,47556,49908\"": "",
    "The bots talk.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "About how many seconds lie between two things said out of the blue in a\n    channel (20 to 3600). Lower is livelier. What is said aloud next to a player\n    follows the same number, a little more seldom.\nDefault:     150": "",
    "Bots answer a hello, a goodbye, a \"ding\", a thanks, a request for a joke or\n    for help and talk about bots - one or two of them, after a few seconds, in\n    General, Trade, the realm's channel or aloud.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "A bot that comes within earshot of a player now and then says something aloud\n    or makes a gesture that suits it; more rarely one within shouting distance\n    yells. When it is time and nobody is near, the next bot that passes speaks.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled": "",
    "With no bot in the player's zone that could say something, the channel of the\n    whole realm is used instead (AiPlayerbot.BroadcastWorldChannelName in\n    playerbots.conf), so a player alone in a zone still reads something.\nDefault:     1 - Enabled\n    0 - Disabled (only the General channel of the zone)": "",
    "Percent of the bots that are a mix of two personalities: mainly one, with a streak of\n    another - the lazy one who keeps talking about food, the pirate who collects things.\n    Such a bot takes about half of what it says from the first, a quarter from the second\n    and the rest from what anybody would say. The others are one thing only, and all the\n    more so. Who is what stays; bots that have a personality already keep it as their first.\n    Pairs that would not be one person (the coward and the hero) do not come up.\nDefault:     65\n    0 - Everybody is one personality only": "",
    "Once in a very long while a bot says something that winks at where all this\n    comes from: the realm that sleeps at night, the day the bots started talking,\n    a tool called AFK Realm. One in this many of the things said out of the blue\n    is such a line; with the default that is one every few evenings.\nDefault:     300\n    0 - Never": "",
    "No Bloodforged gear while it is switched off": "",
    "Bloodforged.Enable = 0 only stops CoA's extra Bloodforged drops; the items stay in some loot tables and in the item store the bots are geared from. Bots wore them on realms without Bloodforged, often with models the client cannot show. While the switch is off, bots are not geared with them, do not put them on, and a re-gear replaces the ones they wear.": ""
  }
}
